19.1 — React Native 0.84 to 0.87

Breaking changes

- React Native 0.82 and 0.83 are no longer supported. Apps on those versions should stay on the latest 19.0.x release. The Nitro version this release requires cannot build against React Native 0.83's iOS target, so the minimum is now 0.84
- Expo SDK 55 is no longer supported, because it ships React Native 0.83. Use Expo SDK 56 or 57, or stay on SDK 19.0.x
- `react-native-nitro-modules` must now be exactly `0.37.1` (19.0.x required `0.35.9`). Upgrade it together with the SDK — a mismatched Nitro version stops the SDK from starting
- Android: the SDK now requires Connect Android SDK 11.1 or newer (the supported range is `[11.1.16-beta, 12.0.0)`), because the bridge uses Connect APIs added in 11.1 and relies on the Android fixes below. With the default config the app picks it up automatically; with `useRelease: true` the app uses Maven Central releases only, so it needs the 11.1 release there
- iOS: the SDK now requires Connect iOS SDK 2.1.51 or newer (the pod floor was 2.1.13). 2.1.51 is the first build with all the iOS fixes below; newer 2.1.x and 2.2.x builds also satisfy it
- The package now requires Node 20.19.4 or newer (it was 20.0.0)

New

- React Native 0.87 support, alongside 0.84, 0.85 and 0.86. Every supported version was verified on a freshly created app: install, native build on iOS and Android, and the SDK actually starting and delivering an event on both platforms
- React Native 0.87 needs Node 22.13 or newer; React Native 0.84 to 0.86 still accept Node 20.19.4 or newer

Upgrading from 19.0.x

- No JavaScript API changed, so your app code stays as it is
- Move to React Native 0.84 or newer and React 19.2.3 or newer (on Expo, SDK 56 or newer), and Node 20.19.4 or newer
- If you pin `Connect.AndroidVersion`, pin 11.1.16-beta or newer; a pin below that now fails at configuration time. Beta versions are on the GitHub-hosted repository only, so with `useRelease: true` pin a Maven Central 11.1 release in that range instead. If you pin `Connect.iOSVersion`, pin 2.1.51 or newer
- Install both packages together: `npm install react-native-acoustic-connect@^19.1.1 react-native-nitro-modules@0.37.1`
- Then run `pod install` in `ios/` (on Expo, re-run `npx expo prebuild`)

Sample apps

- All three sample apps now build on React Native 0.86.3 with React 19.2.3, and the Expo sample targets Expo SDK 57

Bug fixes

- Android: the app now resolves the Connect Android SDK from the same repository as the bridge. Connect betas are published only to a GitHub-hosted Maven repository, which the bridge's own build used but the app's did not, so with the default config an app packaged the newest Maven Central release while the bridge was compiled against a newer beta. On 19.1 that crashed the app with `NoSuchMethodError` when a dialog opened, and logged one on every screen change. `useRelease` now selects the source for both: `false` (the default) uses the beta repository, `true` uses Maven Central releases only, so it needs a Connect Android 11.1 release there
- Android: tap and text-input capture no longer looks up the touched view on the JavaScript thread. React Native's renderer requires that lookup on the UI thread, so debug builds logged an `Expected to run on UI thread!` assertion on every tap, and the lookup raced the renderer while it updated its views. The lookup now runs on the UI thread, and a view removed before it runs is skipped
- Android: `Connect.AndroidVersion` pins now hold. Previously, with push enabled, a pin could be overridden to the newest version in range. A pin outside the supported range now fails at configuration time, with the range in the message
- Android: the postinstall step no longer appends a second `apply from: .../config.gradle` line to `android/app/build.gradle` when the app already applies it
- iOS: screen capture now resolves the react-native-screens screen on display instead of React Native's root view controller. Launch and explicit layout captures no longer send a duplicate, self-referencing screen view. One case remains: switching to a tab that hosts a nested stack still sends one extra screen view
- iOS (arrives with Connect iOS SDK 2.1.49 or newer): while an `Alert.alert` was on screen and screenshots were on, the current screen was posted again about every 0.6 seconds, as a self-referencing screen view with a layout, until the alert closed. That no longer happens. The view controller React Native uses to host an alert is no longer logged as a screen, and the alert is now reported like a native alert: an UNLOAD of the screen, then an alert screen view while it is shown
- iOS, debug builds (arrives with Connect iOS SDK 2.1.51 or newer): React Native's dev loading banner, shown on every Metro refresh, is no longer logged as a screen. Each refresh used to post a self-referencing screen view, a layout and an UNLOAD
- Android (arrives with Connect Android SDK 11.1.11-beta or newer): a signal logged on a screen whose capture is off is now sent at once, instead of waiting until the user opens another captured screen
- Android (arrives with Connect Android SDK 11.1.13-beta or newer): returning to a screen after visiting one whose capture is off now logs the return screen view. It used to be dropped as a duplicate of that screen's earlier visit
- Android (arrives with Connect Android SDK 11.1.16-beta or newer): a password field (`TextInput` with `secureTextEntry`) is now captured as one dot per character. Its typed text used to reach the collector in text-change events
- iOS (arrives with Connect iOS SDK 2.1.46 or newer): with `NumberOfWebViews` set, layouts are now captured for screens that do not contain a WebView. Before, the setting stopped layout capture for those screens, so apps that set it can see more layout messages. Screens with a WebView still capture the layout after the page loads. `CaptureLayoutOn: 0` still turns layout capture off
- iOS (same SDK version): an app's own inherited `webView(_:didFinish:)` and similar delegate callbacks are no longer swallowed by the SDK


